<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Katagum Emirate</h1><p class="st-meta">1807 CE – 1903 CE (approx.) · capital Azare</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An emirate of the Sokoto Caliphate on the western frontier of Bornu, founded by Fulani fighters who once sacked the Bornu capital.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Katagum was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-zaki">Ibrahim Zaki</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> scholar and commander who took up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> in the western borderlands of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kanem-bornu-empire">Kanem-Bornu Empire</a>. In 1808 he joined other Fulani leaders in capturing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu</a> capital, Birni <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngazargamu">Ngazargamu</a>, and in about 1807-1809 he founded the town of Katagum as the seat of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Katagum remained a frontier state facing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu</a>, which recovered under al-Kanemi and pushed back the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a>. The emir ruled through titled officials and district heads on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto</a> model, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a>'s economy rested on farming, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and trade in grain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/natron">natron</a>. It had rivalries with neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadejia">Hadejia</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>British forces occupied Katagum in 1903 and it became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/protectorate-of-northern-nigeria">Protectorate of Northern Nigeria</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a>'s seat later moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azare">Azare</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> of Katagum still reigns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bauchi-state">Bauchi State</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/katagum-emirate.jpg" alt="Katagum Emirate" width="320" height="177"><figcaption>Image: Musaddam Idriss · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Katagum_Emirate_Central_Mosque_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1807 CE – 1903 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Azare</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ibrahim Zaki</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Fulfulde, Hausa, Kanuri</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Emirate of Katagum</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1855&amp;focus=katagum-emirate" aria-label="Katagum Emirate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4964 1720 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Katagum Emirate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5284 1976L5144 1947"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5144 1947h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5284 1976h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5284 1976h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1855&amp;focus=katagum-emirate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

